# Work and trade by job (doc 30): iron, stone, timber and the backs that haul. Smiths, miners, woodcutters,
# crafters, labourers and carters. Plain lines answer each other in parallel.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Smiths

scene tr_smith_temper
when topic=work a.job=smith
a : Quenched it too fast. Cracked right down the tang. | This blade won't hold an edge, whatever I do. | Three days on one hinge. Three days!
b[apprentice] : I told you the oil was too cold. Quietly. To myself.
b : Start again. | Bad iron. | Must be some hinge.
a : Start again, he says. With what coal? | The iron's fine. I'm the problem today. | It's for the chapel door. It has to be perfect.

scene tr_smith_apprentice_bellows
when topic=work a.job=smith b.job=apprentice|child
a : Steady on the bellows. Steady, not wild. | Watch the colour. Cherry red, then pull it. | Hold the tongs in your jaws like you mean it.
b : My legs are tired. | It looks orange to me. | They taste of soot.
a : Your legs will thank you in ten years. | Orange is too hot. Watch again. | Everything here tastes of soot. Get used to it.

scene tr_smith_horseshoes
when topic=work a.job=smith b.job=carter|farmer|herder
a : Your horse has thrown another shoe. | That wheel rim's worn to a whisker. | Bring the plough in before the frost or it'll snap.
b : Can you do it today? | How much for a new one? | It snapped already. Last frost.
a : Today, if the horse behaves. | More than you want, less than a new wheel. | Then you should have brought it in before the last frost.

scene tr_smith_coal
when topic=work|prices a.job=smith
a : Charcoal's gone up again. | The coal's wet. The fire spits. | I need iron and there's none to be had.
b[miner] : Nobody sells iron cheap when the pits are flooding.
b : Everything's up. | Dry it by the forge. | Try the caravans.
a : My prices stay down, though. Somehow. | It is by the forge. It's still wet. | The caravans want coin first.

scene tr_smith_order
when topic=work a.job=smith
a : A lord's man wants a sword by the new moon. Can't be done. | I'm making nails today. Just nails. All day. | Someone ordered a weathervane shaped like a goose.
b : Tell him so. | Nails pay. | A goose?
a : You don't tell a lord's man anything. | Nails pay, nails bore. | A goose. With a crown on.

scene tr_smith_guard_blade
when topic=work a.job=smith b.role=guard
a : Your spear's notched like a saw. What did you hit? | You've been sharpening that wrong. | Leave the sword. Collect it Marketday.
b : A wall. Long story. | I sharpen it the way the sergeant does. | Marketday? I'm on duty.
a : Walls win. | Then the sergeant's wrong too. | Then send a pup for it.

scene tr_smith_heat
when topic=work a.job=smith season=summer
a : Forge in summer. I'm cooked like a ham. | Drank a whole bucket and sweated out two. | Worked with the doors open and still near fainted.
b : Work at night. | Two buckets? | Sit in the river.
a : Then the neighbours complain of the ringing. | Two. I counted. | The river's full of tanners' muck.

scene tr_smith_cinderbrook
when topic=work a.job=smith|miner region=marches
a : Ser Ferro wants picks by the cartload and pays by the spoonful. | The Crown's buyer weighed my iron short again. | Ore's redder from the north seam. Better iron.
b : The city always does. | Scales are crooked. | North seam's cursed, they say.
a : And we always sell. | Everyone knows. Nobody says. | Cursed or not, it rings true.

scene tr_smith_westmarch_farrier
when topic=work a.job=smith region=downs|steppe
a : Every drover on the downs wants their beasts shod before the drive. | Ploughshares, all spring. Ploughshares and hinges. | Somebody brought a cart axle in two pieces.
b : Busy, then. | Good honest work. | Mendable?
a : Busy as a flea. | Honest. Not exciting. | Everything's mendable for a price.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Miners

scene tr_mine_gallery
when topic=work a.job=miner
a : The east gallery's creaking. We need more props. | Water's up to my belly in the low workings. | Struck a good vein today, fat and red.
b[old] : Listen to the creaking. The rock talks before it falls.
b : Tell the overseer. | Bail it, then. | About time.
a : He says it's always creaked. | With what, my mouth? | Don't tell anyone. They'll want a share.

scene tr_mine_lamp
when topic=work a.job=miner
a : My lamp went out down there. Pitch black. | The air's bad in the deep cut. Lamp burns blue. | Lost my lamp in the flood. Have to buy another.
b : How'd you get out? | Don't go down. | Lamps cost.
a : Followed the wall by scent. Slowly. | And lose a day's wage? | Everything costs. Except dying.

scene tr_mine_cough
when topic=work|smalltalk a.job=miner
a : Can't stop coughing. Dust's in me now. | Spat black again this morning. | Pit cough's got old Harl. Three years down and he's done.
b[healer] : Steam and honey. And stay out of the pit a week.
b : Drink something. | Gods. | Poor soul.
a : Ale doesn't help. I tried. | It's just the pit. | He'll go back down. They all do.

scene tr_mine_shift
when topic=work a.job=miner time=dawn
a : Down before the sun, up after it. Never see daylight. | First into the cage again. | Another day under the hill.
b : Spring's coming. Longer days. | Someone has to be first. | Bring a song.
a : Longer days for the rest of you. | Someone always is me. | I'll sing the one about the drowned seam.

scene tr_mine_pay
when topic=work a.job=miner b.job=miner|official|merchant
a : They paid us by the cart, and the carts got bigger. | The overseer docked me for a broken pick. | Wage day, and the tavern's already open.
b : Clever, that. | Your pick, your fault. | Don't spend it all.
a : Clever for them. | The rock broke it, not me. | Half. I'll spend half.

scene tr_mine_luck
when topic=work a.job=miner
a : I left a nail at the shrine before going down. | Never whistle in the pit. Never. | Saw a white rat in the gallery today. Bad sign.
b : Did it help? | Why not? | Or a rat.
a : I came up. It helped. | The rock hears. That's why. | White ones are never just rats.

scene tr_mine_kin
when topic=work|family a.job=miner band=family|spouses
a : I'll be late. They want a double shift. | Don't wait up. The pump's broken. | They're closing the north gallery. Shorter hours.
b : Be careful. Promise. | I always wait up. | Thank the Bright One.
a : I promise. I always do. | I know. Go to bed anyway. | Shorter hours, shorter pay.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Woodcutters

scene tr_wood_felling
when topic=work a.job=woodcutter
a : Felled an oak this morning. Shook the whole hillside. | My axe-head flew off mid-swing. | Wet wood, all of it. Won't burn till next year.
b : I felt it in my bed. | Hurt anyone? | Stack it and wait.
a : That was me. You're welcome. | Only a stump. | Next year I'll be cold, too.

scene tr_wood_harness
when topic=work a.job=woodcutter
a : Dragged three logs home in harness today. My shoulders. | The saw's too blunt to cut butter. | The wolves from House Fell are cutting in our wood again.
b[carter] : I'll take them on the cart, for a cut.
b : Rest them. | Sharpen it. | Our wood? It's the lord's.
a : Rest them, then drag three more. | I did. That was sharp. | The lord's never set paw in it.

scene tr_wood_season
when topic=work a.job=woodcutter season=autumn|winter
a : Everyone wants firewood at once. | Cut till dark, cut tomorrow. | Frozen sap. The axe bounces.
b : Raise your price. | Steady work, at least. | Wait for a thaw.
a : I did. They still want it. | Steady as rain. | The town won't wait.

scene tr_wood_forest
when topic=work|smalltalk a.job=woodcutter
a : Saw a bear's marks on a pine today. High up. | The deep wood's quiet lately. Too quiet. | Found a shrine out in the trees. Old one.
b : Stay out of there. | Bandits? | Who'd build there?
a : I went the long way home. | Maybe. Maybe just winter. | Somebody old.

scene tr_wood_ridgemere
when topic=work a.job=woodcutter|laborer region=ridgemere
a : House Fell's foremen want twenty logs a wolf a day. | Floated the timber down to the yard. Lost two in the rapids. | The shipyard takes every straight trunk we cut.
b : Twenty! | Lost to the sea now. | Ships need masts.
a : Twenty. And they count. | House Fell will dock me for them. | Masts and more masts.

scene tr_wood_carpenter
when topic=work a.job=woodcutter|crafter b.job=crafter|woodcutter
a : I need seasoned ash, not this green stuff. | Bring me cherry, if you find any. | This plank's warped like a dog's leg.
b : Green's what I've got. | Cherry costs. | Wood moves. It's alive.
a : Then I'll wait. | It's for a cradle. | It's dead. It's a plank.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Crafters

scene tr_craft_order_late
when topic=work a.job=crafter
a : The cooper's waiting on my staves and I'm two days behind. | Somebody wants a chest carved with their whole family on it. | I've been sewing harness leather since dawn.
b : Two days is nothing. | Their whole family? | Your jaw must ache.
a : Not to a cooper. | Eleven of them. And a dog. | My jaw's past aching. It's numb.

scene tr_craft_tools
when topic=work a.job=crafter
a : Lost my good awl down a crack in the floor. | The glue pot boiled over and set like stone. | My father's chisels, still sharp after forty years.
b : Pull the boards up. | Smells awful. | Good steel.
a : And have the landlord on me? | Like a glue-boiling. Which it was. | Good steel, good father.

scene tr_craft_apprentice
when topic=work a.job=crafter b.job=apprentice|child
a : Measure twice, cut once. How many times did you cut? | Sand it with the grain, not against it. | That joint's loose. Do it again.
b : Three times. | Which way is the grain? | It's only a little loose.
a : Three cuts, one plank, no shelf. | Look. The lines. Follow them. | A little loose is a broken chair.

scene tr_craft_glass
when topic=work a.job=crafter region=ridgemere
a : Ashcombe glass came out green again. Too much iron. | Blew a bowl that held for one day. | The upper streets want coloured panes for every window.
b : Green's pretty. | One day's something. | Coloured glass. In Ridgemere?
a : Not on an Ashcombe bill. | Not for a mark it isn't. | To look at the rain in colour.

scene tr_craft_cooper
when topic=work a.job=crafter region=rain_coast|lake
a : Every barrel I make goes to the salt house. | Hoops won't sit on these staves. | A leaky barrel came back. My name's burnt on it.
b : Good trade. | Soak them. | Not your best.
a : Steady trade. Never good. | Soaked them a day. | My worst. And my name.

scene tr_craft_tanner
when topic=work a.job=crafter region=downs
a : The hide yard stinks so bad my mate won't sit with me. | Lime's eaten through my pads. | Twelve hides soaked and stretched today.
b : Bathe. | Wear boots. | Busy day.
a : I did. In the river. Twice. | On four paws? | And all twelve smell the same.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Labourers

scene tr_lab_hauling
when topic=work a.job=laborer
a : Hauled stone all morning in harness. Back's like broken pottery. | The foreman wants it done by dark. | Dropped a sack of grain on my paw.
b[old] : Wait till you're older. Then it's like broken pottery all the time.
b : Rub it with oil. | Can't be done. | Ouch.
a : Oil's dear. Wolves are cheap. | That's what I told him. He laughed. | Grain's softer than stone, at least.

scene tr_lab_day_wage
when topic=work a.job=laborer
a : Waited at the hiring post all morning. No one picked me. | Four pennies for a whole day. Four. | Got work on the wall repairs. Steady, for a week.
b : Try the docks. | It's food. | Lucky.
a : Docks want the big ones. | It's half a meal. | A week's a lifetime, this year.

scene tr_lab_foreman
when topic=work a.job=laborer b.job=official|merchant|laborer
a : Is there work tomorrow? | The bricks came short. Again. | My shift's done. Where's my pay?
b : Come early and see. | Count them twice. | Restday. Like everyone.
a : I'm always early. | I did. Twice short. | Restday's three days off.

scene tr_lab_ditch
when topic=work a.job=laborer|farmer place=field|street
a : Digging drains in this. Up to my hocks in mud. | The road's sunk again by the bridge. | Cleared three loads of muck from the gutters.
b : Rather you than me. | They'll want it fixed by market. | Someone has to.
a : Much rather. Pay me double. | Of course they will. | And it's always me.

scene tr_lab_wall
when topic=work a.job=laborer region=cities
a : The council's mending the wall. Wants every back in the city. | Scaffold swayed under me. Didn't look down. | Mortared stones all day. Paws white as flour.
b : Good pay? | Brave. | Like a baker.
a : Council pay. So no. | Brave or daft. | A baker gets bread at least.

scene tr_lab_docks
when topic=work a.job=laborer|sailor place=shore|work region=rain_coast|ser_ferro|lake
a : Unloaded a whole hull of salt before noon. | The crane rope snapped. Barrel went in the water. | They want us on the quay at the tide, not at the bell.
b : No wonder you're tired. | Who pays for it? | The tide doesn't keep hours.
a : Tired and salted. | Not the shipowner. | Nor do we, any more.

scene tr_lab_two_tired
when topic=work|smalltalk a.job=laborer b.job=laborer
a : Why do we do this? | One more load and I'm done. | Race you to the end of the pile.
b : Because we eat. | You said that four loads ago. | You'll lose.
a : Eating's overrated. | I meant it four loads ago. | I'll lose slowly.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Carters

scene tr_cart_road
when topic=work a.job=carter
a : Ruts on the north road deep enough to drown a pup. | Axle cracked halfway up the hill. | Made the run in a day and a half. A record.
b : Somebody should mend them. | Get it to the smith. | Bet the horse doesn't think so.
a : Somebody. Never anybody. | I carried it on my back. | The horse is sulking. He'll live.

scene tr_cart_load
when topic=work a.job=carter
a : Overloaded again. The merchant won't listen. | Half my load was rain-soaked when I got there. | Lost a cask off the back somewhere on the hill.
b[merchant] : Load what I say, cart what I pay for.
b : Then refuse. | Cover it. | Go back and look.
a : And he'll hire someone who won't. | I did. Rain doesn't care. | Someone's found it by now. Lucky them.

scene tr_cart_bandits
when topic=work a.job=carter
a : I don't take the forest road after dark. Not any more. | Two carters were robbed last month on the Accord road. | Always ride with a crossbow on the seat now.
b[guard] : We patrol it. Most days.
b : Wise. | Where? | Can you hit anything with it?
a : Wise and alive. | By the old milestone. | Barely. But they don't know that.

scene tr_cart_horse
when topic=work a.job=carter|herder
a : My old horse is going lame. | Bought a new mare. Bites. | The horse ate my lunch. Bread, cheese, the cloth.
b : Rest her a week. | They all bite at first. | Even the cloth?
a : A week's no money. | Not like this one. | The cloth was the best part, apparently.

scene tr_cart_crossing
when topic=work a.job=carter region=accord|steppe
a : Toll's gone up at the ford. | Waited half a day for the toll keeper to wake. | The caravan shed's full. Had to stack outside.
b : Again? | Lazy beggar. | It'll rain on it.
a : Again. Every season. | Asleep in his booth. | It always does.

scene tr_cart_upper_accord_road
when topic=work a.job=carter|merchant region=upper_accord|accord_crossing
a : Concord seals on every crate now. Takes an hour at the gate. | The road up to the Accord's all switchbacks. Kills the horses. | Hauled a load of manuscripts. Heavier than stone.
b : Rules are rules. | Go slower. | Words weigh, they say.
a : Rules are slow. | Slower than slow? | They weigh in a cart, I'll tell you that.
